.TH LD01 1 "October 2026" "mc6801-tools" "mc6801-tools Manual"
.SH NAME
ld01 \- linker for MC6801 object files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ld01
.RB [ \-dimMPrstyZ ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR script ]
.RB [ \-A
.IR symbol = value ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR textaddr ]
.RB [ \-D
.IR dataaddr ]
.RB [ \-H
.IR heapsize ]
.RB [ \-C \fIcrt\fP]
.RB [ \-L \fIlibdir\fP]
.RB [ \-l \fIlib\fP]
.RB [ \-O \fIlibfile\fP]
.I file
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ld01
combines object files produced by
.BR as01 (1)
and modules from archives made with
.BR ar01 (1)
into an executable.
It resolves references between modules and relocates code and data to
their final addresses.
.PP
Each
.I file
is an object file or an archive.
A module is linked only if the program uses it: starting from the
modules that define the entry point,
.B _main
or
.BR start ,
the linker includes every module that defines a symbol a linked module
refers to.
Archives may appear anywhere on the command line.
.PP
A linker script controls where everything goes: which parts of which
modules go into each output section, the section addresses, and extra
symbols.
Without
.BR \-S ,
the linker uses a built\-in default script, which gives the layout
described under SEGMENTS.
.B \-P
prints it, as a starting point for your own.
See LINKER SCRIPTS.
.PP
The program starts at the first symbol declared with the assembler\(aqs
.B ENTRY
directive.
Linking fails with \(lqno start symbol\(rq if there is none.
.PP
By default the output is an a.out executable with a 32\-byte header,
followed by the text segment, the data segment and a symbol table.
Use
.B \-d
for a plain memory image instead.
.BR objcopy01 (1)
converts a.out files to S\-record, Intel hex, cassette and other formats.
.SH OPTIONS
Single\-letter flags may be turned off again by appending
.BR \- ,
for example
.BR \-s\- .
.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write the output to
.I outfile
instead of
.BR a.out .
.TP
.BI \-T " textaddr"
Set the address of the text segment.
The value is read like a C number: hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
or
.B $
prefix, octal with a leading
.BR 0 ,
and decimal otherwise, so
.BR \-T0x1000 ,
.B \-T\(aq$1000\(aq
and
.B \-T4096
all mean $1000.
(Quote
.B $
from the shell.)
Anything else, such as
.BR 1000x ,
or a value above $FFFF, is an error.
The default is 0.
.TP
.BI \-D " dataaddr"
Set the address of the data segment, written as for
.BR \-T .
Unless
.B \-d
is given, this implies
.BR \-i .
By default the data segment directly follows the text segment.
.TP
.BI \-H " heapsize"
Set the symbol
.B __heap_top
to the end of the program plus
.I heapsize
(written as for
.BR \-T ).
.TP
.BI \-C crt
Link
.BI crt crt .o
as a start\-up module, for example
.B \-Cstart
for
.BR crtstart.o .
.TP
.BI \-l lib
Search the archive
.BI lib lib .a\fR.\fP
.TP
.BI \-L libdir
Add
.I libdir
to the directories searched for
.BR \-C ,
.B \-l
and
.B \-O
files.
.TP
.BI \-O libfile
Link
.IR libfile ,
looking for it in the search directories.
.TP
.B \-d
Write a headerless memory image: the text segment followed by the data
segment, with no header or symbols.
Implies
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B \-i
Mark the executable as having separate instruction and data spaces.
.TP
.B \-m
List the modules linked on standard output.
Each line shows the module name and its source file.
.B +
marks a linked module and
.B \-
an archive member that was not needed.
.TP
.B \-M
Print the layout, each output section with its address and size and the
input sections in it, then all symbols linked, with module, value and
type, on standard output.
.TP
.BI \-S " script"
Use the linker script in
.I script
instead of the default script.
.TP
.BI \-A " symbol" = value
Define
.IR symbol ,
as if the script began with
.IB symbol " = " value ;\fR.\fP
The script can use it, and it takes the place of the script\(aqs
.B PROVIDE
for the same symbol.
.TP
.B \-P
Print the default linker script on standard output and exit.
The script includes the values of
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-D ,
.B \-H
and
.BR \-d .
Linking with it
.RB ( \-S )
gives the same output as linking without a script.
No input files are needed.
.TP
.B \-Z
Make it an error for the script not to place some input (see Orphans
under LINKER SCRIPTS).
.TP
.B \-r
Instead of linking, write the input objects to
.I outfile
as an
.BR ar01 (1)
archive.
.TP
.B \-s
Do not include a symbol table in the output.
.TP
.B \-t
Trace the modules as they are read.
Must come before the input files.
.TP
.B \-y
Write an extended symbol table.
.TP
.B \-v
Print a version message and exit.
.SH LIBRARY SEARCH
Files named by
.BR \-C ,
.B \-l
and
.B \-O
are searched for in the
.B \-L
directories, most recently added first.
There is no default library directory.
If the file is not found in any of them, the name is opened as given,
relative to the current directory.
Up to five
.B \-L
directories may be given.
.SH SYMBOLS
The linker defines these symbols, which programs may import:
.TP
.BR __btext ", " __etext
Start and end of the text segment.
.TP
.BR __bdata ", " __edata
Start and end of the data segment.
.TP
.B __end
End of all data, including uninitialized data and common blocks.
.TP
.B __heap_top
End of the program plus the
.B \-H
value.
.PP
.BR __btext ,
.BR __etext ,
.BR __bdata ,
.B __edata
and
.B __end
are always written to the executable\(aqs symbol table, unless
.B \-s
is given.
.BR objcopy01 (1)
uses them to find the load addresses.
.SH LINKER SCRIPTS
A script is a list of commands.
Comments are written
.B /* ... */
or start with
.BR # .
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ENTRY(reset)

SECTIONS
{
    .text 0xE000 :
    {
        crt0.o(LOC0)
        *(LOC0 LOC1)
        *(COMMON0 COMMON1)
    }
    .data 0x0080 :
    {
        *(LOC*)
        *(COMMON)
    }
    __stack_top = 0x00FF;
}
.fi
.RE
.SS Commands
.TP
.BI ENTRY( symbol )
Start the program at
.IR symbol ,
before any
.B ENTRY
in the object files.
.TP
.BI OUTPUT_FORMAT( format )
.B aout
(the default) or
.BR binary ,
a memory image as with
.BR \-d .
.TP
.BI SECTIONS " { ... }"
Output sections and symbol assignments, in order.
.TP
.IB symbol " = " expression ;
Define a symbol.
.TP
.BI PROVIDE( symbol " = " expression );
Define it only if a linked module imports it and no module defines it.
.TP
.BI HIDDEN( symbol " = " expression );
Define a symbol that only the script can use.
.SS Output sections
.nf
\fIname\fP [\fIaddress\fP] : [ALIGN(\fIn\fP)] [SUBALIGN(\fIn\fP)] { \fIstatements\fP }
.fi
.PP
Without an
.IR address ,
a section starts at the current address
.RB ( . ),
which is where the previous section ended.
.B ALIGN
rounds the start up to a multiple of
.IR n .
.B SUBALIGN
pads each input section in it to a multiple of
.I n
bytes.
.PP
Statements inside a section, in order:
.TP
.IB file ( sections )
Add the matching input sections.
.TP
.BI ONEPAGE " { statements }"
Keep the contents within one 256\-byte page: if they would end on a
later page than they start, they move to the start of the next page.
.TP
.IB symbol " = " expression ";\fR, \fP" PROVIDE ", " HIDDEN
Define symbols, often using
.BR . ,
the current address.
.TP
.BI ". = " expression ;
Move the current address forward.
.SS Input sections
Each linked module has two input sections for each location counter
.IR n :
.BI LOC n\fR,\fP
its code and data, and
.BI COMMON n\fR,\fP
its common blocks.
.B .text
means
.BR LOC0 ,
.B .data
means
.BR LOC3 ,
and
.B COMMON
means all the common blocks.
.PP
.I file
matches the object file name, with or without its directory; archive
members are named
.IB archive : member\fR.\fP
Both
.I file
and the section names may use the wildcards
.B *
and
.BR ? .
Each input section goes to the first statement that matches it.
A statement adds its input sections in module order, and each module\(aqs
in counter order.
.SS Orphans
Input that no statement matches is added at the end of the last output
section for its kind (counters 0 and 1, or the others), with a warning.
.B \-Z
makes it an error.
.SS Expressions
Expressions use C operators and numbers written as for
.BR \-T .
A
.B K
suffix multiplies by 1024.
The functions are
.BI ADDR( section )\fR,\fP
.BI SIZEOF( section )\fR,\fP
.BI LOADADDR( section )\fR,\fP
.BI ALIGN( n )
(the current address rounded up),
.BI ALIGN( expression ", " n )\fR,\fP
.BI DEFINED( symbol )\fR,\fP
.B MAX
and
.BR MIN .
Symbols may be used before they're defined; the linker repeats the
layout until nothing changes.
.SS Symbols
Symbols defined by a script are absolute.
They satisfy imports from any location counter.
A plain assignment to a symbol that a module also defines is an error.
.SS a.out output
The output sections up to the first one that can take input from
location counters 2 to 15 form the a.out text part, and the rest the
data part.
The output is marked as having separate instruction and data spaces if
the data part doesn\(aqt start where the text part ends.
Common blocks at the end of the data part are its uninitialized data.
The header\(aqs total memory size reaches
.B __heap_top
if the script sets it.
.SS Not yet supported
.BR MEMORY ,
.BR AT ,
.BR NOLOAD ,
.BR KEEP ,
.BR FILL ,
.BR ASSERT ,
.BR INCLUDE ,
and S\-record or Intel hex output.
.SH SEGMENTS
This is the layout of the default script.
The assembler has 16 location counters.
Counters 0 and 1 are linked into the text segment, and counters 2
through 15 into the data segment.
If counter 2 would cross a 256\-byte page boundary, it is moved to
the start of the next page.
.B .TEXT
selects counter 0, and
.B .DATA
and
.B .BSS
select counter 3.
.SH EXAMPLES
Link a start\-up module, two objects and a library, loading at $434C:
.PP
.RS
.B ld01 \-Cstart \-lnum \-T0x434c \-o main.aout main.o screen.o
.RE
.PP
Produce a raw memory image for an EPROM at $E000:
.PP
.RS
.B ld01 \-d \-T0xe000 \-o rom.bin start.o prog.o
.RE
.PP
Start a linker script from the default layout, edit it, and use it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B ld01 \-P \-T0x434c > mc10.ld
.B ld01 \-S mc10.ld \-Cstart \-lnum \-o main.aout main.o screen.o
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Undefined symbols are reported as
\(lqundefined symbol:
.IR name \(rq.
The exit status is 0 on success and non\-zero on error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR as01 (1),
.BR ar01 (1),
.BR objdump01 (1),
.BR objcopy01 (1)
.PP
The mc6801\-tools User\(aqs Manual
.RI ( mc6801\-tools\-manual.pdf ).
.SH BUGS
Symbol names in the a.out symbol table are cut to 8 characters.
.SH HISTORY
Derived from
.B ld86
in Bruce Evans\(aq C compiler, as maintained in the dev86 package.
